questions:
This workspace supports a follow-up study to our recent JAMA publication demonstrating that over 5% of the U.S. population carries pathogenic germline variants (PGVs) in cancer susceptibility genes. The goal of this study is to define gene-specific cancer prevalence, spectrum, burden, lifetime risk, comorbidity and the risk associated with autosomal recessive (AR) genes among carriers in an unselected, population-based cohort.
We specifically aim to:
(1) Quantify cancer and multi-cancer prevalence among carriers;
(2) Compute cancer prevalence and spectrum among carriers of PGVs;
(3) Compute lifetime risk for carriers with a significantly early cancer onset;
(4) Compare age distributions between carriers with/without cancer diagnoses;
(5) Identify co-occurrence count and cancer prevalence in both mono-allelic AR gene carriers and autosomal dominant gene carriers.
(6) Compute cancer burden among single and multi-gene carriers;
(7) identify novel or unexpected gene–cancer associations;

approaches:
We will leverage the rich phenotypic and genomic data of participants with diverse ancestries in AoU to 
1) develop, test and apply advanced analysis methods to quantify the genetic architecture in complex traits by modelling both shared and population-specific genetic variant effects through intensive simulations;
2) analyse GWAS data for a wide range of complex traits that are shared between and specific to different ancestries in real data analyses;
3) validate results of cross-ancestry fine-mapping and polygenic prediction in our models and compare the findings to other methods.

questions:
In this study, we will expand on previous research to examine the role of social environment in shaping biological risk profiles for Alzheimer's disease. Using the All of Us data, we will pursue the following aims:
1. To test whether neighborhood us associated with elevated levels of systemic inflammation and key cardiometabolic markers linked to Alzheimer's risk;
2. To assess if metabolic phenotype predicts increased Alzheimer's risk in a population based cohort stratifying for sex, menopausal status, and social context measured as neighborhoods;
3. To examine whether systemic inflammation and metabolic dysregulation influences the associative relationship between neighborhood attributes and Alzheimer's disease;
4. To explore potential social and biological interactions that may shape increased Alzheimer's risk across the socioeconomic gradient.

