approaches:
We plan to employ causal analysis methods to determine the relationship between GLP-1 use and alcohol consumption. We will define multiple comparison groups based on GLP-1 status at the time of completing the Lifestyle survey, which includes detailed information on the frequency and volume of alcohol consumption. Comparison groups will include those:

With no documented GLP-1 use, but whose health history is compatible with GLP-1 prescription;
Who begin GLP-1 use after completing the survey;
Who began GLP-1 prior to completing the survey.

Individuals in comparison groups will be weighted using inverse propensity weighting (IPW) to control for differences in group compositions, using features derived from self-reported surveys as well as the EHR. We will then compare the frequency and volume of alcohol consumption derived from the Lifestyle survey across all groups.

The proposed aims are:
to train longitudinal multivariate models that identify latent biological aging axes across the menopause transition;
to determine whether these axes are better aligned with chronological age, menopausal stage, or time relative to the final menstrual period;
to interpret the biological composition of the axes by identifying the strongest contributing biomarkers and clinical variables;
to test whether the axes are associated with later-life outcomes, including cardiometabolic risk, bone health, physical-function decline, frailty-related measures, and mortality where available; and
to prepare the model for future validation in independent cohorts, including and local Singaporean clinical cohorts.

