Our course-based project explores how genetic, environmental, clinical, and social factors jointly influence health outcomes in diverse U.S. populations. Students will investigate a range of scientific questions, including:
- how air pollution (PM2.5) relates to emergency visits across urban and rural areas;
- how polygenic risk scores (PGS) and clinical features improve prediction of Alcohol Use Disorder across ancestries;
- how medication data can be represented to better support disease phenotyping with machine learning;
- how genetic variants affect risk of immune therapy-related kidney injury using causal machine learning.
- how mitochondrial DNA variation contributes to chronic disease risk.
These questions are important for advancing precision health and reducing health disparities. Each project is designed to engage real-world health data through a scientific lens, with the goal of generating insights relevant to both research and public health.

